  "text": "We are given:\n\n- A **cylindrical container** with:\n  - **Height = 10 cm**\n  - **Radius = 6 cm**\n  - Each **sphere** has:\n    - Radius = **1 cm**\n    - So, the **diameter = 2 cm**\n\n- **35 spheres are visible** from the **top** and **not covered** by any other spheres.\n\nWe are to find the **smallest possible number of spheres** that can fill the **container**.\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Understand the visible spheres\n\nFrom the top, **35 spheres are visible** and **not covered** by any other. That means:\n\n- **At least 35 spheres are inside the container**, and the **top part** of the container is visible.\n\nThis implies that **the bottom part of the container** must be **filled with spheres**, and **the top part must be covered by 35 spheres**.\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Total volume and packing\n\nTo find the **smallest number of spheres**, we need to **find the minimum number of 1 cm radius spheres** that can fill a cylindrical container of height 10 cm and radius 6 cm.\n\nThe volume of each sphere is:\n\n$$\nV_{\\text{sphere}} = \\frac{4}{3} \\pi r^3 = \\frac{4}{3} \\pi (1)^3 = \\frac{4}{3} \\pi \\text{ cm}^3\n$$\n\nLet $ N $ be the number of spheres. Then:\n\n$$\nV_{\\text{container}} = \\frac{4}{3} \\pi (6^3) = \\frac{4}{3} \\pi (216) = 288 \\pi \\text{ cm}^3\n$$\n\nSo, the total volume of the container is:\n\n$$\nV_{\\text{container}} = 288 \\pi \\text{ cm}^3\n$$\n\nNow, the volume of 35 spheres is:\n\n$$\nV_{\\text{visible}} = 35 \\cdot \\frac{4}{3} \\pi = \\frac{140}{3} \\pi \\text{ cm}^3\n$$\n\nWe are told that the **visible spheres are not covered**, so:\n\n$$\nV_{\\text{visible}} = \\frac{140}{3} \\pi \\approx 46.67 \\pi \\text{ cm}^3\n$$\n\nThis suggests that the **top part of the container** is covered by **35 spheres**, and the **bottom part** is filled with some number of spheres.\n\n---\n\n### Step 3: Determine the number of spheres at the bottom\n\nLet $ x $ be the number of spheres at the bottom.\n\nThen:\n\n$$\nV_{\\text{bottom}} = x \\cdot \\frac{4}{3} \\pi\n$$\n\nSince the total volume is $ 288 \\pi $, we have:\n\n$$\n\\frac{4}{3} \\pi (x + 35) = 288 \\pi\n$$\n\nDivide both sides by $ \\pi $:\n\n$$\n\\frac{4}{3} (x + 35) = 288\n$$\n\nMultiply both sides by 3:\n\n$$\n4(x + 35) = 864 \\Rightarrow x + 35 = 216 \\Rightarrow x = 181\n$$\n\nSo, **181 spheres** are at the bottom, and **35** are visible.\n\n---\n\n### Step 4: Check if this is the **smallest** number\n\nWe need to ensure that **no more than 181 spheres** can fit in the container.\n\nBut we already assumed the **top** part is filled with 35 spheres, and the **bottom** with 181. Since the container is 10 cm tall and 6 cm in radius, and the spheres are 1 cm in radius, it's possible to place **more than 181** spheres in the bottom, but we are to **find the smallest possible number** of spheres.\n\nSo the **smallest possible number** of spheres that can fill the container is:\n\n$$\n\\boxed{181}\n$$",
